## 8.1 HTML: the structure

HTML defines what each part of the page means.

For example:

- `<header>` contains the navigation.
- `<main>` contains the primary content.
- `<section>` separates the major parts of the showcase.
- `<article>` groups related information, such as a venture or impact card.
- `<button>` creates an interactive control.
- `<a>` creates a link to another page or section.
- `<footer>` contains the closing project information.

Semantic HTML makes the structure easier to understand for both people and assistive technologies.

### Example: navigation link

```
<nav aria-label="Main navigation">
  <a href="#journey">Journey</a>
  <a href="#ventures">Ventures</a>
  <a href="#impact">Impact</a>
  <a href="#sources">Sources</a>
</nav>
```

The link `href="#journey"` points to the element with `id="journey"`.

When the visitor clicks it, the browser moves to that section. CSS adds smooth scrolling, unless the visitor's operating system requests reduced motion.

## 8.2 CSS: layout and responsive design

CSS styles the HTML elements.

The project uses CSS Grid for the hero and card layouts, Flexbox for navigation and horizontal alignment, and media queries for smaller screens.

For example:

```
.hero {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1.12fr 0.88fr;
  gap: 58px;
  align-items: center;
}
```

This creates two columns. The first receives slightly more space than the second, so the text and introduction remain prominent while the portrait card still has room to stand out.

The responsive rule is:

```
@media (max-width: 800px) {
  .hero {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }

  .timeline {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }

  .venture-grid,
  .impact-grid,
  .lessons,
  .sources-grid {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}
```

When the viewport becomes narrower than 800 pixels, the hero becomes one column and several card layouts become more compact.

A second breakpoint at 480 pixels adjusts navigation, timeline cards, the portrait placeholder, and spacing for smaller mobile screens.

## 8.3 JavaScript: structured data

One of the explicit technical requirements is that at least one section should be generated from an array or structured list.

The project goes further: both the timeline and venture cards are generated from JavaScript arrays.

Instead of writing four separate timeline cards manually in the HTML, the data is stored separately:

```
const milestones = [
  {
    year: "2003",
    title: "An everyday problem sparks an idea",
    short: "A missed train connection reveals an opportunity.",
    detail: "The observation about empty seats inspired the idea."
  },
  {
    year: "2006",
    title: "The company takes shape",
    short: "The service launches as Covoiturage.fr.",
    detail: "The original idea became a company and a working service."
  }
];
```

This is a simplified excerpt; the actual project contains four milestone objects with longer descriptions.

Each object represents one milestone. The properties are:

- `year`: the time period shown on the card.
- `title`: the milestone heading.
- `short`: the brief description displayed on the card.
- `detail`: the longer description shown when the card is selected.

The data is separate from the page-generation logic, making it easier to maintain.

### How the timeline is generated

The code uses the array's `forEach()` method:

```
milestones.forEach((item, index) => {
  const button = document.createElement("button");

  button.className = "milestone";
  button.type = "button";

  button.innerHTML = `
    <span class="year">${item.year}</span>
    <h3>${item.title}</h3>
    <p>${item.short}</p>
  `;

  timeline.appendChild(button);
});
```

Here's what happens:

1. `forEach()` visits each object in the array.
2. `item` refers to the current milestone.
3. `document.createElement("button")` creates a new HTML button.
4. The class and type are assigned.
5. A template literal inserts the current object's content into the button.
6. `appendChild()` adds the button to the timeline container.

The actual project also attaches a click handler to each button, sets the appropriate accessibility attributes, and updates the detail panel.

### Why this approach is useful

Suppose you want to add another milestone.

With a data-driven implementation, you add another object to the array. The loop creates the card automatically.

You don't have to copy and rewrite a separate HTML block, reducing repetitive code and making the content more consistent.

This is a simple example of separating data from presentation.

## 8.4 The interaction: clicking a milestone

The timeline uses JavaScript event listeners to respond to clicks.

When a milestone is clicked, the code:

1. Removes the active styling from all milestone buttons.
2. Changes their `aria-pressed` attributes to `false`.
3. Applies the active styling to the selected button.
4. Sets its `aria-pressed` attribute to `true`.
5. Updates the detail panel's year, title, and description.

The essential logic looks like this:

```
button.addEventListener("click", () => {
  timeline.querySelectorAll(".milestone").forEach(card => {
    card.classList.remove("active");
    card.setAttribute("aria-pressed", "false");
  });

  button.classList.add("active");
  button.setAttribute("aria-pressed", "true");

  detailYear.textContent = item.year;
  detailTitle.textContent = item.title;
  detailCopy.textContent = item.detail;
});
```

This demonstrates three fundamental JavaScript concepts:

- DOM manipulation: Changing elements on the page.
- Event handling: Running code in response to a click.
- State representation: Showing which milestone is currently selected.

The active state is represented through the `active` CSS class and `aria-pressed` attribute rather than a separate framework or database.

## 8.5 The interaction: expandable venture cards

Each venture card includes a button that reveals additional information.

The code toggles a CSS class:

```
const isOpen = article.classList.toggle("open");
```

If the card did not have the class, `toggle()` adds it. If it already had the class, `toggle()` removes it.

The CSS determines whether the additional description is visible:

```
.venture-extra {
  display: none;
}

.venture.open .venture-extra {
  display: block;
}
```

The button's label and `aria-expanded` value are also updated so the control communicates whether its content is open.

This is a lightweight alternative to building a more complicated modal or separate details page.

