# 10. User experience, accessibility, and usability

The project should work for visitors who are not familiar with the entrepreneur, and it should be usable on both a desktop and a phone.

## Navigation and readability

The navigation provides direct links to all major sections. The content is divided into short descriptions, cards, and headings so visitors do not need to read the whole page to find relevant information.

The main call to action, “Explore the journey,” directs visitors to the timeline. This creates a clear path from the introduction into the central content.

## Keyboard and assistive technology support

The implementation includes several useful accessibility practices:

- Native buttons are used for clickable timeline and venture controls.
- Native links are used for navigation and external sources.
- `aria-label` provides accessible names for certain interface elements.
- `aria-pressed` communicates which timeline button is selected.
- `aria-expanded` communicates whether a venture card is open.
- `aria-live="polite"` allows updates to the selected milestone's details to be announced by compatible assistive technologies.
- `:focus-visible` styles help keyboard users identify the focused element.
- The reduced-motion media query avoids forcing smooth scrolling and transitions on users who prefer reduced motion.

These are helpful foundations, not a guarantee of complete accessibility. The finished page should still be tested with a keyboard and, if possible, a screen reader.

## Responsive behaviour

The page uses media queries to adapt to different viewport sizes.

| Screen size  | Expected behaviour                       |
| ------------ | ---------------------------------------- |
| Desktop      | Two-column hero and four-column timeline |
| Tablet       | One-column hero and two-column timeline  |
| Small mobile | One-column cards and reduced spacing     |

The actual transition depends on viewport width rather than the device name. A small browser window on a laptop may trigger the same layout as a phone.

## Performance

The website is deliberately lightweight:

- No JavaScript framework is required.
- No build tools need to run.
- No API requests are needed for the main interactions.
- There are no external JavaScript libraries.
- Most decorative elements are CSS-based.

This reduces setup complexity and makes the project easier to run locally.

